Aphids are small, soft-bodied insects that feed on certain types of plants. They obtain their energy by consuming plant sap, which is produced by the plants themselves. This means that aphids are dependent on plants for their energy and do not produce their own food through photosynthesis.

Based on this information, we can conclude that aphids are not producers (A) because they do not produce their own food through photosynthesis. They are also not decomposers (B) because they do not break down dead organic matter.

Aphids are herbivores, which means they consume plants as their primary source of energy. As they consume plants, they are being eaten by other organisms, such as ladybugs or lacewings, which are predators of aphids. This means that aphids are not second-level consumers (D) because they are not being eaten by other organisms.

Therefore, aphids are first-level consumers (C) because they consume plants as their primary source of energy and are being eaten by other organisms.

